{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790959981,"nonce":"svzjDe+pr8wC+H5CyFqIaBtaYNQ=","title":"Opening Lua compiles Zyntax's Lua runtime library in every process","message":"Measured 2026-10-02 on the gpu plugin fixture (debug; Zyntax crates are opt-level 3 in every profile): `caribou_lua::Lua::prepare` takes 106–154 ms of about 380 main-thread samples, at `Session::open`, before the program's main runs. Most of it is `zyntax_lua::open_host` (lowering and compiling the library through `TieredRuntime::compile_typed_program`, and `zyntax_lua_capi::host_state`); about 40 ms more is caribou's own helper chunk (`host::run_chunk_n(HELPERS)`), loaded and compiled the same way.\n\nAny program whose roots hold a .lua file pays it, whether or not it loads Lua early. Zyntax tracks the same shape for Python (a440a60, the exception prelude lowered for every program) and ZynML (507f983). The library and the helper chunk are the same in every process, so they could ship lowered or compiled. The fix is Zyntax's; its session has been told.","files":null}]}